# ORION Phase 10D — Architecture Specification

Version: 1.0

Status: Approved Architecture Specification

---

# Purpose

Phase 10D completes the operational workflow of ORION by introducing the Mission Planning experience that precedes Mission Control.

The objective of this phase is **not** to redesign the architecture nor to introduce additional autonomous intelligence.

Instead, Phase 10D extends the existing Digital Twin platform so that an operator can perform an entire agricultural mission from initial field creation through mission deployment using a complete simulation workflow.

This phase transforms the current Mission Control demonstration into an end-to-end operational demonstration suitable for presentations, validation sessions and investor demonstrations.

---

# Design Principles

The architecture established during Phases 8–10 remains fully preserved.

No subsystem changes responsibility.

No execution logic moves to the frontend.

No planning logic is duplicated.

No business logic is added to the UI.

The Digital Twin remains the only operational source of truth.

Mission execution continues to be coordinated by the existing backend architecture.

---

# Mission Definition — The Central Contract

Phase 10D does **not** connect UI planning actions directly to execution.

Every operator planning action creates or edits a single, formal artifact: the
**Mission Definition**. The Mission Definition is the central contract that
represents a complete mission lifecycle, from user definition through Digital
Twin execution.

```
User Planning Experience
        │
        ▼
Mission Definition        (created/edited by the UI, persisted)
        │
        ▼
Planning Intelligence     (Planning Core — existing core/ pipeline)
        │
        ▼
Mission Package           (generated routes, resources, timeline, warnings)
        │
        ▼
Mission Execution Simulation
        │
        ▼
Mission Control
```

Expanded conceptual flow:

```
Field
        ▼
Preparation
        ▼
Mission Designer
        ▼
Mission Definition   ← the contract every planning screen writes to
        ▼
Planning Core        ← computes the plan (backend only)
        ▼
Mission Package      ← execution-ready artifact for the Digital Twin
        ▼
Mission Library      ← persists reusable definitions/templates
        ▼
Digital Twin
        ▼
Mission Control
```

Principles:

1. **Mission Definition is the central contract.** The UI creates and edits a
   Mission Definition; it never plans. The Planning Core processes the
   definition; the result is a Mission Package consumed by the Digital Twin.
2. **Foundational, not throwaway.** Phase 10D completes the demonstration, but
   the Mission Definition / Mission Package contract and its persistence are
   designed to evolve into the Phase 11 commercial system without rebuilding the
   foundations. The persistence layer is replaceable.
3. **Later sub-phases only enrich the contract.** 10D.3–10D.7 add fields to the
   Mission Definition (geometry, zones, obstacles, crops, operation parameters,
   fleet selection, products, scheduling metadata); they do not introduce new
   execution paths.

The **Mission Definition** is owned by Mission Planning (design-time data). The
**Mission Package** and all runtime state remain owned by the Digital Twin
(runtime truth). No duplicated source of truth is introduced.

