# Existing Modules

The following modules remain unchanged.

Mission Control

Digital Twin

Hive

HAL

Simulation Core

Route Planner

Risk Engine

Decision Engine

Analytics

Replay

History

Their responsibilities remain exactly as defined during previous phases.

---

# Architectural Relationships

Mission Planning produces mission definitions.

Mission definitions are submitted to the backend.

The backend computes operational plans.

The Digital Twin reflects the resulting operational state.

Mission Control visualizes Digital Twin state.

Replay reconstructs historical Digital Twin states.

Analytics visualizes Digital Twin historical metrics.

At no point does the frontend compute operational decisions.

---

# Data Ownership

Mission Planning

Owns

* Editable mission definitions

Digital Twin

Owns

* Runtime operational state

Hive

Owns

* Mission orchestration

HAL

Owns

* Hardware abstraction

Replay

Owns

* Historical reconstruction

Analytics

Owns

* Operational metrics

No ownership overlaps are introduced.

---

# Frontend Responsibilities

The frontend may

* Display information
* Collect operator input
* Persist editable missions
* Submit intents
* Configure visualization
* Display analytics
* Display replay

The frontend never

* Plans routes
* Allocates drones
* Balances resources
* Schedules execution internally
* Computes optimization
* Generates autonomous decisions

---

# Backend Responsibilities

The backend remains responsible for

* Planning
* Resource management
* Swarm coordination
* Mission orchestration
* Runtime synchronization
* Simulation
* Digital Twin updates
* Replay generation

No backend decision logic is moved to the frontend.

---

# Digital Twin Integration

All operational visualization continues to originate exclusively from the Digital Twin.

The UI communicates only through

* REST APIs
* WebSocket telemetry
* Replay endpoints
* Snapshot endpoints

The UI never communicates directly with

* Hive
* HAL
* PX4
* MAVLink
* ROS2

---

# Phase Objective

At the conclusion of Phase 10D, ORION shall provide a complete end-to-end operational demonstration capable of simulating the full agricultural workflow without requiring physical drones or hardware stations.

The resulting platform shall accurately represent the intended commercial workflow while preserving all architectural boundaries established in previous phases.

This architecture serves as the final demonstration architecture prior to Phase 11, where the commercial production platform will begin development.
